ow the pandemic  can drive you up the wall even if it  does that too  a recent study indicates that the  coronavirus might also be able to  directly damage the brain  facting it in ways that could explain  certain long-covet symptoms  autopsies on people who died from the  disease have shown that covid-19 can  trigger inflammation in the brain  indirectly even if the virus itself  isn't detected there but some experts  think South Korea could also play a more  direct role in neurological symptoms  so what would the virus do exactly if it  reached the brain  that was the question at the heart of  the study the researchers tweeted  knowing that the virus was  neuro-invasive we wanted to study how  different cell types of the brain  respond to the viral Invasion we used  neuroimmune brain organoids containing  microglia and modeled live viral  infection brain organoids are often  called mini brains by the Press but they  aren't really brains they are  three-dimensional clumps of culture  tissue made up of the cell types found  in the human brain  so how did these organoids react to  being infected in the lab with sasko V2  spoiler alert not very well  the researchers found that microglia and  astrocytes had distinct responses that  shared features observed in  neurodegenerative diseases and activated  microglia tripled the engulfment of  post-synaptic Termini of neurons say  what  let's break that down a little to get  what the researchers mean you first have  to understand what microglia are  microglia cells are immune cells that  clean up molecular debris in the brain  and go after pathogens that try to  infect it they also support the repair  of damaged neurological tissue at least  they do under normal circumstances  but a direct infection with a sales cov2  is anything but normal  it caused the microglia in the organoids  to kind of flip out the researchers  observe that the immune cells started  destroying structures they once  encouraged to grow  synapses  so in