ady set some money aside  before covid-19 and once the pandemic  hit they used those funds to buy food to  be shared with the other families  most of the members are casual laborers  while the self-employed have also seen  their incomes dropping off in the last  two years  I joined a saving group where I took a  loan and opened my hotel business  that was new for me  but after a while I paid the loan back  and took another one and used it to buy  a small house  now I want to keep saving more money so  that I can upgrade it  foreign  savings groups help them boost their  loaning capacity  it wasn't easy to borrow money from  financial institutions during this time  as the interest on loans was high  plus they needed collateral which most  of the individuals did not have  the savings groups serve as collective  collateral with the members vouching for  each other  [Music]  during covid our savings group used to  get requests to make masks and PPE and  as we also teach tailoring in this group  we took on those orders we took a loan  from the Sako Co-op and we were able to  deliver and after we got paid we  returned the loan money  another big benefit is that through the  savings groups we were able to access  loans from the credit union  the money provided enables us to work on  tenders  when we take on orders for making soap  for example and also when we need money  for machines  shining hope for communities or Shopko  is one of the other organizations that  the groups have helped to attract  funding from  it provides grants and Loans to help  local traders in poor areas to recover  from the covert related slump  Shin introduces the women to the table  banking system and also trains them in  financial literacy  doesn't mean those efforts have enabled  the women to develop a joint saving  culture in the community  I'll tell you out of 15 000 members here  70 percent are women women are the most  powerful people in this community yes  and you know in terms of also businesses  we had women who are you know  preparing how do you call the illicit  Brew changa around right now they have  left those you know risky businesses and  they are into a proper and legal  businesses for the women the chance for  a new life  since the outbreak of the pandemic  experts around the world have grappled  with the question of how best to fight  the Coronavirus  what's the consensus on the best way to  treat SARS kovi 2. as with many other  diseases research remains ongoing here's  an overview of the methods developed so  far  early in the pandemic doctors and  healthcare workers desperately sought  ways to treat patients badly affected by  covert 19. life-saving interventions  included methods used to treat other  severe respiratory diseases among them  supplementary oxygen and artificial  ventilation  severe covid-19 is often caused by the  body's own immune system if it goes into  overdrive and begins attacking a  patient's own tissue it can be calmed by  anti-inflammatory drugs especially a  class of steroid-based medicines like  one called dexamethasone  as the pandemic progressed drug  developers focused on other ways to  treat the disease before it grew too  severe like the protective antibodies  produced by people who had survived an  encounter with covid-19 which latch onto  the virus to disable it using modern  techniques they can be copied and  produced on a large scale  these monoclonal antibodies or mabs  could be given to high-risk patients  early on  this could give a patient's immune  system more time to catch up before the  virus could replicate massively  spiraling out of control a final class  of medicines used to help prevent  infected patients from developing severe  disease is called antivirals they took  longer to develop but are now widely  available  antivirals work by interfering with  viral replication which in the end  lowers the number of viruses that are  produced  that also gives a patient's immune  system time to catch up in the race to  learn about the Invader and fight it off  before the illness can take a turn for  the worse  [Music]  we also talked to zandratzizek director  of The Institute of Medical virology at  the University Hospital Frankfurt and  professor of medical virology at the  Goethe University  antiviral drugs are her area of  expertise  [Music]  for a long time we focused on developing  vaccines to protect people from SARS  cov2  effective drug treatments took a back  seat is that still the case  yeah we now fortunately have a few  antiviral drugs that can also be  administered orally as a tablet one is  malnu piravir which inhibits enzymes in  the virus it inhibits the polymerase  activity that the virus needs to  replicate the other drug is a protease  inhibitor an enzyme that's the drug most  of us know under the brand name of  paxlovid  the drug that was used at the beginning  of the pandemic REM deserveer is now  being used more often again but it's a  drug that has to be administered  intravenously  the other issue is that all these drugs  are only effective if they're taken at  the beginning of the infection meaning  within five days of developing symptoms  after that we tend to turn to drugs that  modulate the immune system like  cortisone which many people have heard  of  steroids in other words  is drug development generally more  difficult than vaccine development and  how did that play out in the case of  SARS Co V2  yeah  we were lucky because we were able to  draw on what are called repurposed drugs  those are drugs that were developed for  other diseases or that had reached a  certain stage of development  is one example when it became apparent  that certain drugs were effective  against SARS cov2 that made it possible  to eliminate some stages in their  clinical development  that is drugs that already existed but  had not yet been given official  clearance  [Music]  you're also researching treatments  against SARS cov2 what approach are you  taking  we're pursuing a variety of approaches  for example there are weak spots on the  coronavirus that we're trying to Target  with enzymes and the same holds for the  points on the host cell that the virus  attaches to there's also what's called  high throughput screening which involves  screening huge chemical libraries  containing many thousands of substances  to see if you get a hit meaning it's  something that shows biological activity  you can also work in a targeted Way by  studying the virus and how it replicates  and identify targets  that could be a protein that the virus  needs to replicate for example which you  then try to Target  we've been pursuing various strategies  and have had some promising results  but they're all still in the early stage  of research  at the cell culture model stage did it  will take a long time before they're  ready to be tested in humans  so the pandemic might be over by then  we'll probably have reached a different  stage of things by then but SARS cov2  isn't going away there will always be  people who become seriously ill for  example people who are immunosuppressed  or elderly people some will still need  drug treatments five years or ten years  from now another strategy that we're  pursuing is that we're also trying to  develop what are called broad-spectrum  antivirals  those would be drugs that wouldn't be  limited to just a single virus like SARS  cov2 but might be effective against all  coronaviruses for example  we already know that coronaviruses have  the potential to lead to pandemics we've  seen it several times we want to act  prophylactically and be ready for the  future  that's why we're hoping that one day if  a SARS cov3 or MERS 2 becomes a problem  will already have an effective drug that  we can start using right away  Professor cesack thank you for the  interview thank you  the pandemic was also a very difficult  time for doctors  in our my covert section we meet one in  Colombia  the pandemic negatively impacted  everything the health system the economy  our mental health Society everybody was  affected  my name is Stella Navarro I'm an  intensive care physician in Medellin I  only realized by chance that I was  infected  I was working my shift I wasn't feeling  ill  I was eating dinner when I noticed that  I had no taste and I knew that's a  common covered symptom  I had an existing problem with my spinal  column and covered worsened the pain it  had to be treated I needed a nerve root  block  for covert patients being in intensive  care was psychologically challenging  the emotional impact was considerable  these people were already very  frightened  brutal and if you're in an ICU you know  the situation is serious and the fact  that no visitors were allowed made it  even worse  so all of us working in intensive care a  lot was lost but a lot was gained we  managed to pull through we did it but it  was a very hard situation A catastrophic  one there were moments of hopelessness  the experience was traumatic and had to  be processed  on a personal level we felt responsible  we weren't Heroes we were just doing our  job I don't know it was very exhausting  there's a sense now that we need to make  up for that time recover the time we  gave to all these people for ourselves  we're out of debt but I don't think it  will ever be paid back  [Music]  we hope we've managed to answer some of  your questions about the current  pandemic situation and what the path  forward may be  next week we'll take a closer look at  the phenomenon of long covert until then  goodbye and look after yourselves  foreign  [Music]

